    "body": "<h2 id=\"before\">Before anything else: agree on the rules</h2><p>You can be curious and cautious at the same time. Negotiation is the conversation that happens before an activity, when everyone can think clearly and decline without pressure. Discuss interests, hard limits, possible concerns, physical accessibility, communication preferences, and what happens if someone wants to stop. An earlier yes never guarantees a later yes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"words\">Safe words are useful—not magical</h2><p>The traffic-light system is simple: green means comfortable continuing, yellow means pause and check in or adjust, red means stop. Agree on ordinary language too. A clear “stop,” withdrawal of consent, distress, freezing, or inability to respond always warrants stopping, even if a special word has not been spoken. For activities where speech might be difficult, agree on an unmistakable nonverbal signal and have a way to observe it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"capacity\">Consent requires the capacity to choose</h2><p>Never treat sleep, significant intoxication, fear, or confusion as consent. Check for medication, fatigue, injury, or health conditions that could affect communication or safety without demanding anyone disclose private medical history. If a person cannot reliably consent or signal, do not proceed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"risk\">Plan for risks, not just intentions</h2><p>Avoid practices that obstruct breathing, restrict circulation, or leave someone unable to exit or call for help. Restraint and impact can cause serious injury; learning from qualified in-person educators is preferable to improvising from internet instructions. Have emergency access, a phone, and a plan to stop immediately. Never leave a restrained person alone.</p>\n<h2 id=\"aftercare\">Aftercare is personal, not a mandatory script</h2><p>Aftercare describes what people may need after an intense experience: water, quiet, a blanket, reassurance, space, or a practical check-in. Ask instead of assuming. Some people want closeness; others want solitude. Follow up later, especially if either person feels unsettled, and respect boundaries after the activity ends.</p>\n<h2 id=\"repair\">When something goes wrong</h2><p>Stop, assess immediate safety, and seek appropriate medical help for injury or concerning symptoms. Later, discuss what happened without minimizing the impact or pressuring anyone to resume. Repeated boundary violations are not a communication style. They are a reason to disengage.</p>\n<h2 id=\"checklist\">The five-minute pre-scene checklist</h2><p>Can everyone freely say no? Have you agreed on the specific activity and its limits? Can everyone signal a pause or stop? Are risks and emergency access understood? What support might each person want afterward? If you cannot answer one of these, the next step is a conversation—not an experiment.</p><h2 id=\"real-world\">What this looks like in real life</h2><p>Imagine two people agree on a scene but one becomes unusually quiet. The responsible response is to stop and check on them, not to wait for a particular word. Silence is not proof of enjoyment.</p><p>Negotiation should include who can initiate, which activities require a fresh question, whether any equipment is involved, and how to handle unexpected discomfort. A “maybe” is not a yes. Written notes help memory but never override what someone says in the moment.</p><p>Aftercare can include a later message the next day. Ask what each person wants rather than treating cuddling or debriefing as mandatory. A partner who wants solitude still deserves respect.</p><h2 id=\"questions-to-ask\">Three questions to take with you</h2><ol><li>What does this topic mean to me personally, without pressure to adopt a label?</li><li>What information, boundaries, or support would make it safer to discuss?</li><li>What would I choose to learn next, even if I never decide to try it?</li></ol><p>There is no deadline for curiosity. You are allowed to leave a question unanswered.</p><h2 id=\"deeper-dive\">A deeper dive</h2><p>These distinctions help turn abstract vocabulary into informed, freely chosen conversations.</p><h3>Negotiation in practice</h3><p>A practical negotiation has three layers. First, talk about interests without expecting agreement. Second, specify what is on the table for this particular occasion, including what is excluded. Third, decide how you will notice and respond to discomfort. A partner can decline the entire conversation or request more time. A respectful response to uncertainty is curiosity, not persuasion.</p><h3>What a check-in sounds like</h3><p>Short, neutral questions often work better than a dramatic interrogation: “Comfortable continuing?” “Would you like to change anything?” “Do you want to stop?” Allow an answer that disappoints you. A person who has trouble speaking, looks disoriented, or seems unable to respond is not providing ongoing consent. Stop and prioritize their wellbeing.</p><h3>Why experience is not immunity</h3><p>Someone may have years of experience and still misunderstand another person's limits. Skills with equipment do not replace interpersonal judgment. Do not treat certifications, community reputation, or confidence as proof that an activity is risk-free. Ask how someone handles mistakes and what they do when a partner stops.</p><h3>Aftercare is a negotiation too</h3><p>The word aftercare can obscure how different people are. Some want a glass of water and to go home; others prefer quiet companionship, a message later, or time to reflect. It is better to ask what support is welcome than to insist on a ritual. Neither person owes physical affection as part of aftercare.</p><p class=\"fine\"><a class=\"text-link\" href=\"#/worksheets?mode=safety\">Open the matching private worksheet</a> (answers stay in this browser tab).</p>",
