    "body": "<h2 id=\"labels\">Labels are maps, not marching orders</h2><p>A kink role is shorthand for an experience, style, or relationship someone enjoys. It is not a personality diagnosis, a contract, or proof of compatibility. Two people may use the same label to mean very different things. Ask what it means to them before assuming.</p>\n<h2 id=\"caregiver\">Daddy Dom and caregiver dynamics</h2><p>A Daddy Dom is generally an adult dominant who values a nurturing, guiding, or protective style. Some use affectionate titles and rituals. This does not inherently imply an actual family relationship or involve minors; all participants must be consenting adults. Caretaking language does not excuse control over healthcare, money, or friendships.</p>\n<h2 id=\"service\">Service submissive</h2><p>Service-oriented submission often centers on chosen acts of helpfulness, ritual, organization, or attentiveness. Service should be specific and appreciated, not an unlimited expectation of unpaid labor. Discuss workload, gratitude, and the right to decline.</p>\n<h2 id=\"brat\">Brat and brat tamer</h2><p>A brat may enjoy playful resistance, banter, or testing a negotiated script. A brat tamer may enjoy meeting that energy with structure and humor. Neither label permits ignoring actual limits. The most useful question is: what counts as play, and what counts as a genuine no?</p>\n<h2 id=\"primal\">Primal dynamics</h2><p>Primal is a broad term for instinctive, spontaneous, or less formal roleplay. It may emphasize movement, sound, or energy rather than titles and protocols. Spontaneous does not mean unplanned consent; boundaries remain essential.</p>\n<h2 id=\"rope\">Rope bunny, rigger, and rope bottom</h2><p>A rope bunny or rope bottom enjoys the receiving role in rope-related activities, while a rigger commonly manages the rope. Rope can involve nerve, circulation, fall, and positional risks. Avoid copying complex ties from pictures; seek qualified hands-on education and never assume someone can safely tolerate a position.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ownership\">Pleasure slave, Master/slave, and ownership language</h2><p>Some adults choose intense language to describe devotion, service, or authority. Such terms are negotiated relationship language, not literal property rights. Any arrangement must preserve personal autonomy, consent, access to support, and the ability to leave.</p>\n<h2 id=\"fit\">Finding a role that fits</h2><p>Try asking: do I enjoy leading, receiving guidance, caretaking, service, playful challenge, or changing roles? What emotional need might this meet? Which words feel welcoming, and which do not? You are allowed to enjoy an experience without adopting its label.</p><h2 id=\"real-world\">What this looks like in real life</h2><p>A caregiver-style title may mean nurturing structure to one person and playful role language to another. Ask about concrete expectations instead of relying on a label to do the communicating.</p><p>Compatibility is not a chart matching one role to another. It depends on mutually welcome activities, aligned boundaries, communication habits, time availability, and willingness to renegotiate.</p><p>A role can evolve. Someone may prefer service during one period of life and switching later. No one owes a partner a fixed identity forever.</p><h2 id=\"questions-to-ask\">Three questions to take with you</h2><ol><li>What does this topic mean to me personally, without pressure to adopt a label?</li><li>What information, boundaries, or support would make it safer to discuss?</li><li>What would I choose to learn next, even if I never decide to try it?</li></ol><p>There is no deadline for curiosity. You are allowed to leave a question unanswered.</p><h2 id=\"deeper-dive\">A deeper dive</h2><p>These distinctions help turn abstract vocabulary into informed, freely chosen conversations.</p><h3>A wider glossary</h3><p>A top typically directs an activity; a bottom receives it. These terms describe what happens, not necessarily who holds authority in the relationship. A switch enjoys different roles in different contexts. A mentor may share knowledge without being a romantic partner. A protector or caregiver emphasizes reassurance and responsibility. A sadist or masochist may enjoy consensual pain-related dynamics, but those words alone do not explain anyone's limits.</p><h3>Role versus responsibility</h3><p>Being called a dominant does not make someone an expert at safety. Being called a submissive does not make someone obligated to obey. A service dynamic can be meaningful without requiring unpaid domestic labor. Caregiving language is not permission to manage someone's medications, employment, or social relationships. Ask for specifics and make sure the arrangement can be ended without retaliation.</p><h3>Titles are personal</h3><p>Words like Sir, Ma'am, Daddy, Mommy, Master, or Mistress carry different meanings in different communities and relationships. Some people love them; others find them uncomfortable or culturally loaded. There is no universal requirement to use a title. People can also enjoy a fantasy role privately without wanting it to shape everyday life.</p><h3>Try a conversation before a label</h3><p>Instead of asking “What are you?”, ask “What kinds of interaction do you enjoy, what is only fantasy, and what do you avoid?” Those questions reveal more than a checklist of identities. If your preferences change, the old label does not become a contract.</p><p class=\"fine\"><a class=\"text-link\" href=\"#/worksheets?mode=roles\">Open the matching private worksheet</a> (answers stay in this browser tab).</p>",
